@* Principle 9 – Users can use different input modalities beyond keyboard *@

<h2>Alternatives to dragging and complex gestures</h2>
<p>
    Dragging, pinching, and multi-finger swipes need fine motor control. Every one of them needs a simple
    alternative, such as a single tap or click. Rank these topics by dragging them with a mouse, <em>or</em> use the
    Move up and Move down buttons, which work with touch, keyboard, voice control, and switch devices.
</p>
<ol class="sortable" id="rank-list" aria-label="Topics ranked by interest">
    @foreach (var topic in new[] { "Screen readers", "Color contrast", "Keyboard testing", "Captions" })
    {
        <li draggable="true" data-name="@topic">
            <span>@topic</span>
            @* The visible text ("Move up") comes first in the accessible name, so voice
               control commands like "click Move up" still work. *@
            <span class="item-actions">
                <button type="button" class="button-secondary" data-move="up">Move up<span class="visually-hidden">: @topic</span></button>
                <button type="button" class="button-secondary" data-move="down">Move down<span class="visually-hidden">: @topic</span></button>
            </span>
        </li>
    }
</ol>

<h2>Prevent accidental activation, and offer undo</h2>
<p>
    People with tremors, or anyone on a bumpy bus, will sometimes tap the wrong thing. Destructive actions should
    be easy to undo. Native buttons also activate on <em>release</em> (the <code>click</code> event), so you can slide
    your finger off a button to cancel. Avoid triggering actions on <code>mousedown</code> or <code>touchstart</code>.
</p>
<ul class="sortable" id="file-list" aria-label="Your files">
    <li><span>lab1-notes.txt</span> <button type="button" class="button-danger" data-delete>Delete<span class="visually-hidden"> lab1-notes.txt</span></button></li>
    <li><span>project-plan.docx</span> <button type="button" class="button-danger" data-delete>Delete<span class="visually-hidden"> project-plan.docx</span></button></li>
    <li><span>screenshot.png</span> <button type="button" class="button-danger" data-delete>Delete<span class="visually-hidden"> screenshot.png</span></button></li>
</ul>
<div class="toast" id="undo-toast" hidden>
    <span id="undo-text"></span>
    <button type="button" id="undo-button">Undo</button>
</div>

<h2>Visible labels match the code (label in name)</h2>
<p>
    Voice control users say what they see, for example “Click Search”. If the accessible name in the code is
    different from the visible label, the command fails. The accessible name should <em>contain</em> the visible text,
    ideally at the start.
</p>
<div class="compare">
    <div class="example example-good">
        <h3>Do: match the visible text</h3>
        <p><button type="button">Search</button></p>
        <code-sample kind="good" label="Matching label">
            <button type="button">Search</button>
        </code-sample>
    </div>
    <div class="example example-bad">
        <h3>Don't: override it with a different name</h3>
        <p><button type="button" aria-label="Find items in catalog">Search</button></p>
        <code-sample kind="bad" label="Mismatched label">
            <button type="button" aria-label="Find items in catalog">Search</button>
        </code-sample>
        <p>Saying “Click Search” won't work, because the button's name is “Find items in catalog”.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>Motion-activated features need a button too</h2>
<p>
    “Shake to undo” or “tilt to scroll” is impossible for someone whose device is mounted to a wheelchair,
    and it can be triggered by accident. Always provide a regular control, and let users turn motion actions off.
</p>
<code-sample label="Motion with a button alternative">
    <button type="button" id="undo">Undo</button>   <!-- always available -->
    <script>
        undoButton.addEventListener("click", undo);
        if (settings.shakeToUndo) {                   // user can turn this off
            window.addEventListener("devicemotion", detectShake);
        }
    </script>
</code-sample>

<h2>Large enough targets</h2>
<p>
    WCAG 2.2 requires targets of at least <strong>24 × 24 CSS pixels</strong> (level AA) and recommends
    <strong>44 × 44</strong> (level AAA). All buttons on this site have a minimum height of 2.75rem (44px).
</p>
<div class="compare">
    <div class="example example-good">
        <h3>Do: generous targets</h3>
        <div class="target-demo">
            <button type="button" class="button-secondary">Previous</button>
            <button type="button" class="button-secondary">Next</button>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="example example-bad">
        <h3>Don't: tiny targets close together</h3>
        <div class="target-demo">
            <button type="button" class="button-secondary tiny-target">Prev</button><button type="button" class="button-secondary tiny-target">Next</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@section Scripts {
    <script src="~/js/input-modalities.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
}
